<body><h1>Methodology</h1>
<p>Estimates are produced from a stratified sample; margins of error are reported at the
ninety percent confidence level.</p>

<p>These control estimates are produced from a stratified probability sample of housing units
drawn independently in each geography. Sampling strata are defined by county and by tenure,
and the sample is allocated across strata in proportion to the square root of stratum size, so
that small geographies retain enough sample to support a published estimate. Weighting
proceeds in three stages: a base weight equal to the inverse of the selection probability, a
non-response adjustment computed within weighting class, and a post-stratification adjustment
to independent population controls.</p>
<p>Every published estimate carries a margin of error at the ninety percent confidence level.
The margin of error is derived from a successive-difference replication variance estimate with
eighty replicate weights. Users combining estimates across geographies should not add margins
of error directly; the covariance between estimates drawn from the same sample is not zero and
the resulting interval would be too wide. The published replicate weights allow a correct
variance estimate for any derived quantity.</p>
<p>Estimates are suppressed where the unweighted sample count falls below the disclosure
threshold, or where the estimated coefficient of variation exceeds the publication ceiling.
A suppressed cell is published as a reserved code rather than omitted, so that a machine
reading the file can distinguish a suppressed value from a missing one. The reserved codes and
their meanings are listed in the accompanying data dictionary and repeated in the column
descriptions of every distribution.</p>
<p>Revisions follow a published schedule. A scheduled revision replaces the estimate for a
prior reference period when new source data become available; an unscheduled revision corrects
an error and is announced in the changelog with a revision class of correction. Both kinds of
revision preserve the identifier of the affected dataset, so a consumer holding a prior
vintage can determine what changed without re-downloading the whole product.</p>
<p>Questions about methodology, including requests for the replicate weights or for the
variance estimation code, should be directed to the data desk. The methodology document is
maintained alongside the product and is versioned with it.</p>

